<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdur Rahman Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1840–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Iron Emir · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdur Rahman Khan, the 'Iron <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a>', ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> from 1880 to 1901 after the Second Anglo-Afghan War. With British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> and control of his foreign policy, he built a standing army, crushed tribal revolts, annexed Badakhshan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazarajat">Hazarajat</a> and forcibly converted Kafiristan. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durand-line">Durand Line</a> of 1893 fixed his border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, and his centralised state shaped modern Afghanistan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdur-rahman-khan.jpg" alt="Abdur Rahman Khan" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frank A. Martin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amir_Abdur_Rahman_Khan_of_Afghanistan-cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abdur Rahman Khan, a Pashtun, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in the 1840s, the only son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-afzal-khan">Mohammad Afzal Khan</a> and grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai dynasty</a>. He spent most of his youth at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>. When Dost Mohammad died on 9 June 1863 after naming Sher Ali Khan his successor, Afzal Khan rose in revolt in the north, beginning a struggle among Dost Mohammad's sons that lasted nearly five years. Abdur Rahman fled across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> after Sher Ali grew suspicious of him.</p><p>After Sher Ali's death he asked the Russians for leave to enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> as a claimant, and was at first refused. They allowed it after Yaqub Khan's abdication and the British occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. He annexed Badakhshan and soon held all of Afghan Turkestan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>At a durbar on 22 July 1880 the British recognised him as Amir, promising arms and money and aid against unprovoked foreign aggression, provided he aligned his foreign policy with theirs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayub-khan">Ayub Khan</a>, a son of Sher Ali, defeated his troops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> that July but was beaten there by General Frederick Roberts on 1 September 1880. Backed by British arms and money, Abdur Rahman consolidated his hold over the country, repeatedly facing revolts such as those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilzai">Ghilzai</a>. One source counts over 40 rebellions during his reign.</p><p>His government was a military despotism, resting on a well-appointed army, a widespread system of espionage and officials subservient to him. He also held open courts for petitioners. A British official called his rule a reign of terror, and up to 100,000 people are said to have been judicially executed in his 21 years. He moved troublesome <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilzai">Ghilzai</a> tribes to northern areas populated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> and Tajik communities. In 1895 and 1896 he directed the invasion of Kafiristan and the conversion of its people to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and the region was renamed Nuristan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1893 Mortimer Durand negotiated with him the agreement that fixed the frontier between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durand-line">Durand Line</a>. Habibullah Khan confirmed it in 1905 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah Khan</a> in the Treaty of Rawalpindi in 1919.</p><p>Abdur Rahman died on 1 October 1901 in his summer palace, and his son Habibullah Khan succeeded him. He wrote an autobiography and treatises including one on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> hold mixed views of him. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a> remember him as a ruler who began modernisation and kept the country from Russian occupation, though he depended on British arms and money to stay in power. His great-grandson Prince Abdul Ali Seraj said he united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> under one king and one flag.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abdur Rahman Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Second Anglo-Afghan War of 1878 to 1880, <b>Abdur Rahman Khan</b>, the 'Iron Emir', centralised power, crushed revolts and annexed the Hazarajat, while Britain controlled foreign policy.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-afghanistan">Emirate of Afghanistan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1859 the Emirate of Afghanistan made Badakhshan a vassal, and around 1881 <b>Abdur Rahman Khan</b> annexed it fully.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-badakhshan">Principality of Badakhshan</a></li></ul></section>
